# Getting started

## Install

Add Basin to your project:

```sh
cargo add basin
```

Basin works on plain `Vec<f64>` out of the box. Linear-algebra backends are
opt-in. For example, to use the latest supported
[nalgebra](https://nalgebra.rs/) backend (required by some solvers, see
[Solvers](../solvers/)):

```sh
cargo add basin --features nalgebra
```

The moving aliases are `nalgebra`, `ndarray`, and `faer`.
Exact version features are also available for nalgebra 0.32–0.35, ndarray
0.15–0.17, and faer 0.22–0.24. The deprecated `nalgebra_latest`,
`ndarray_latest`, and `faer_latest` features remain as synonyms. When several
releases of one backend are enabled, Basin implements each independently.
The package MSRV is Rust 1.87; nalgebra 0.35 and `nalgebra` require Rust
1.89.

In Basin 2.0, no features are enabled by default. Enable `problems` with
`cargo add basin --features problems` to use the standard test-function corpus
in `basin::problems`. Basin 1.x enables this feature by default; on that release
line, `default-features = false` excludes it.

## A simple example

Implement `CostFunction` for your objective. Add a `Gradient` impl when the
solver needs derivatives (gradient descent does; Nelder–Mead does not). Then
hand the problem, a solver, and an initial state to the `Executor`.

```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, FirstOrderState, Gradient, GradientDescent};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Rosenbrock;

impl CostFunction for Rosenbrock {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Vec<f64>) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((1.0 - x[0]).powi(2) + 100.0 * (x[1] - x[0].powi(2)).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Gradient for Rosenbrock {
    type Gradient = Vec<f64>;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &Vec<f64>) -> Result<Vec<f64>, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![
            -2.0 * (1.0 - x[0]) - 400.0 * x[0] * (x[1] - x[0].powi(2)),
            200.0 * (x[1] - x[0].powi(2)),
        ])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = GradientDescent::new(1e-3).with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-6);
    let state = FirstOrderState::new(vec![-1.2, 1.0]);

    let result = Executor::new(Rosenbrock, solver, state)
        .max_iter(50_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    println!("x = {:?}", result.param());
    println!("f = {}", result.cost());
    println!("stopped: {:?}", result.report.termination);
}
```

## Seeding from a point

`Executor::new` takes a fully-built state, so you control the initial iterate.
For the common case (start at a point with the solver's natural defaults),
`Executor::from_start` takes the bare starting vector and builds the state for
you, so you never name the concrete state type:

```rust
use basin::{Executor, GradientDescent};

let solver =
    GradientDescent::new(1e-3).with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-6);
let result = Executor::from_start(Rosenbrock, solver, vec![-1.2, 1.0])
    .max_iter(50_000)
    .run()
    .unwrap();
```

Most solvers support `from_start`, including `CmaEs` and `BoundedCmaEs`: the
starting point supplies the mean, and the solver configures the initial scale.
Solvers that sample a box or need an explicit population, such as PSO, GA, DE,
and random search, require an explicit state. The scalar minimizers `Brent`,
`BrentDerivative`, and `GoldenSection` also require a state because they start
from a bracket. Use `Executor::new` for those solvers.

## Convergence and run limits

Configure numerical convergence on the solver. Configure iteration, evaluation,
and time limits on the executor, along with targets, stagnation stops, and
application conditions:

```rust
use basin::{Executor, GradientDescent};

let solver = GradientDescent::new(1e-3)
    .with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-6)
    .with_relative_cost_change_tolerance(None);
let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, x0)
    .max_iter(50_000)
    .max_cost_evals(100_000)
    .run()
    .unwrap();
```

Optional tolerances accept a finite nonnegative scalar or `None`. Repeated
setters replace the same test; distinct enabled tests usually combine with OR.
Nelder–Mead's enabled simplex size and cost-spread tests combine with AND.
Algorithm-specific defaults and safeguards still apply. Read each solver's
rustdoc for the norm and scaling used by a test.

Defaults vary by solver. In particular,
[`NelderMead`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nelder_mead/struct.NelderMead.html#convergence)
enables paired simplex size and cost-spread tests by default in Basin 2.0,
including its projected variant. Absolute and relative tolerances work together
within each test. An unchanged best vertex alone does not establish collapse,
and collapse on a flat objective or after clipping does not establish
stationarity. See the API reference and
[Basin 2.0 migration guide](../migrating-to-v2/#nelder-mead-convergence-defaults) for settings,
scaling limits, and complete opt-out. Basin 1.x retains its opt-in behavior.

Choose tolerances for the scale of your objective and parameters. An absolute
gradient threshold changes meaning when you rescale the problem. For least
squares, the
[`LevenbergMarquardt` convergence guide](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/levenberg_marquardt/struct.LevenbergMarquardt.html#convergence)
shows how to select orthogonality and relative model-reduction tests through
existing setters. Adding a relative check leaves any absolute check enabled
unless you explicitly disable it with `None`.

Budgets are checked after initialization and between iterations, so evaluation
limits can be exceeded within an iteration. `result.report.termination` reports why the run
ended. Reaching a budget or reporting a numerical stall does not establish
convergence or rule out an accurate solution. Inspect the returned solution
alongside the reason. For an application condition, use `stop_when(|state| ...)`,
returning `Some(basin::ApplicationStop::new("my_stop"))` to stop or `None` to continue.

Basin 2.0 removes the former `terminate_on` facility and abbreviated tolerance
setters. See [Migrating to Basin 2.0](../migrating-to-v2/#stopping-conditions).

